This is the classic old Seven Bridges of Konigsberg problem:

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Bridges_of_K%C3%B6nigsberg#:~:text=The%20Seven%20Bridges%20of%20K%C3%B6nigsberg,bridges%20once%20and%20only%20once.

https://wild.maths.org/bridges-k%C3%B6nigsberg-0#:~:text=In%20the%20eighteenth%20century%20the,yourself%2C%20using%20the%20picture%20below.

https://old.maa.org/press/periodicals/convergence/leonard-eulers-solution-to-the-konigsberg-bridge-problem#:~:text=The%20healthy%20economy%20allowed%20the,the%20famous%20mathematician%20Leonard%20Euler.

Given:
* The town's layout in 1735 as described in the third link above;
* The seven bridges " " " " " " " " ";

Produce:
* A program providing (exhaustive) empirical proof that there is no path that can be taken to walk across all of the bridges exactly once and end up at home.

I think this would be a good exercise in Rust for me, although it is really a mathematical proof rather than a programming problem.  It might make a good takehome interview problem for particularly intellectually-curious candidates.

Extra credit:
* Suppose one of the bridges collapses.  Is the conclusion still the same, or might there now be a way to traverse all of the bridges exactly once?  Does it matter which bridge collapses?

* Suppose one new bridge is added (between a specific pair of the existing nodes).  Is the conclusion still the same?  Is the conclusion the same no matter which pair of nodes you pick?

* Suppose one of the fixed bridges is replaced by a see-saw bridge.  If you approach the lower side of the see-saw, you may walk across it ... but if you approach the higher side, you cannot cross.  In this case, though, if you do cross you are constrained to return *back* across the see-saw at some point during your walk, i.e. unlike every other bridge which must be crossed *once*, the see-saw must be crossed *twice* (it is considered impolite by the locals to leave the see-saw in a different state than when you started).  And it must be crossed in the opposite direction, since that is the only way the lower side of the see-saw will be available to you (assume that no one else crosses the see-saw during your walk).  Is the conclusion still the same?
